WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.819
Gearbox vibration builds during steady cruise
and is also present in a ground check. The

00:00:06.819 --> 00:00:13.589
inputs are the mission computer log, vibration
from two independent sensors and a separate

00:00:13.589 --> 00:00:20.133
ground recording. Finding the cause requires
comparing those recordings with the setup,

00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:22.690
data quality and bearing geometry.

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:30.551
In the AI Assistant settings I select
OpenVINO. Its server runs on this computer. I

00:00:30.551 --> 00:00:38.641
choose Qwen3 Coder 30B from the served model
list and connect. The indicator shows a local

00:00:38.641 --> 00:00:39.630
connection.

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:46.006
I add the flight setup, quality notes and
machine record to the Knowledge Base. RAG

00:00:46.006 --> 00:00:52.486
retrieves relevant passages and their source
revisions during the investigation. The Agent

00:00:52.486 --> 00:00:58.390
uses the documented sensor roles and bearing
geometry when comparing measurements.

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:05.834
I send one Agent request: compare the flight
and ground recordings, test gearbox and

00:01:05.834 --> 00:01:13.632
sensor-installation explanations and export an
evidence-backed PDF. I specify the measurement

00:01:13.632 --> 00:01:19.670
plan up front. The Agent handles opening files
and running the analyses.

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:27.107
The live chat shows what the Agent is doing:
importing recordings, retrieving documents and

00:01:27.107 --> 00:01:33.216
reviewing data quality. It creates and
connects analysis widgets on the canvas,

00:01:33.216 --> 00:01:39.711
taking over the manual work of opening files,
configuring tools and assembling their

00:01:39.711 --> 00:01:40.330
results.

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:48.358
The Agent compares vibration, speed and torque
in early and late flight windows. It reads the

00:01:48.358 --> 00:01:55.575
flight spectra and measured-encoder order
analysis, then uses the ground recording as an

00:01:55.575 --> 00:01:58.610
independent check with its own clock.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:06.113
Both sensors in both recordings support the
approximately 107.4 Hz outer-race family, with

00:02:06.113 --> 00:02:12.270
five harmonic matches in all four analyses.
The result weakens a single-sensor

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:17.400
installation explanation and helps focus the
physical inspection.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:24.194
During report review the operator asks for a
clear distinction between the measured bearing

00:02:24.194 --> 00:02:30.025
signature and a physical fault. The Agent
posts the revised assessment in chat. The

00:02:30.025 --> 00:02:33.960
recurring signature helps focus the physical
inspection.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:41.532
The Agent collects findings, plots,
measurement tables and source references in a

00:02:41.532 --> 00:02:49.324
PDF. An engineer can trace each interpretation
back to its evidence. The link beside the

00:02:49.324 --> 00:02:52.600
video opens the same exported report.
